  "reason": "The records collectively disclose the essential processing and strengthening logic. Multiple refractory-HEA studies use high-energy mechanical alloying under argon followed by short vacuum SPS consolidation, including milling-time studies showing solid-solution formation, plastic-deformation-induced refinement, densification, and hardness near 675 HV. Other records directly connect nanocrystalline or refined grains to Hall–Petch strengthening and combine this with lattice-strain solid-solution hardening and milling-generated defects. Particularly relevant AlCoCrFeNi evidence shows severe deformation and defect accumulation can alter the FCC/BCC balance, while closely related Co-Cr-Fe-Ni-Ti-containing literature attributes strengthening to atomic-size-induced lattice distortion. Although the exact 900–1000 °C schedule, 83/17 FCC/BCC fraction, and precise titanium stabilization argument are not stated together, an expert could plausibly combine the prior teachings to propose the later intervention and mechanism."
